The home screen
29a-sd-tcl-the-home-screen
The home screen is a page of tiles; each tile starts one thing: a tool, the Terminal, or a program you added. Arrows, Home and End move between the tiles, a letter jumps to the next tile that begins with it, / opens a window that finds a tile by any part of its name (type to narrow the list, Enter moves to the tile), Page Down and Page Up go to the next and the previous group, and Enter or the space bar starts the tile. A tile that opens a sub-menu has a Back tile; Esc and Backspace go back one page.
Esc on the top page, F10, Alt and a title's letter, or a click on the top row open the menu bar: File (sign out), Go (back, the Terminal, switch account, find a tile, start the tile), View (refresh, the Recent group, the theme), AI (ask the AI, when it is on) and Help (this page, the keys). Each entry shows its key: Alt-T the Terminal, Ctrl-W switch account, Ctrl-R the Recent group, F6 the AI line, Ctrl-T the next color theme (kept for you: every page starts in it), Ctrl-K the keys of this screen. F5 builds the page again. Ctrl-Q signs out after asking, unless you typed HOME at the prompt: then Ctrl-Q goes back to that prompt, and Sign out is Alt-X (or its File entry or tile).
Every answer of the AI has a Follow up button: it applies nothing, and your next request carries that question and answer (the line says AI follow-up>, and the preview shows them in full before Send). It is used once, and is dropped after ten minutes or when you leave the page.
The page starts nothing by itself: a tile runs the command its owner gave it, in your account, through your own gates.
